A||||19||GTMike||how reliable is your fiero||||1||
Z||000000||GTMike||07-07-2001||05:26 PM||||are fiers reliable?  say an 87 gt with 43,000 miles.   what problems do you run in to.  at what miles do things start going wrong?    any water pump or timeing chain problems? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Bluebottlejunky||07-07-2001||05:33 PM||||I would say, it all matters was the engine and everything on the car maintained? Sure It would prolly have really good reliable rating.  Mine is pretty good, it seems have worked out the bugs.<P>------------------<BR>Hello, My name is Mike and I am addicted too fiero's...PLEASE dont help me.<BR>85'GT 4sp<BR>AIM: Fierofanatic2001||||reg||1
Z||000002||kempo||07-07-2001||05:37 PM||||I have a Black '87 Fiero GT / 5 Speed with 129,999.3 miles.  Oil has been changed religously.  I have not run into any problems so far.  I just got it to pass emissions with a home tuneup of an air filter, plugs, and for what it's worth, rotor and distribution cap.  Engine still runs strong, with no problems.  Every once in a while, maybe one time out of 10, she'll fire up with a little puff of smoke, but that's all.  Does not leak at all, but runs rich.  <P>From what I've heard going around, normal Fiero wear on the 2.8L engine, at around 100k miles, the rod will start knocking.  Hence, rod knocks, get the 3.4L engine.  It's really all up to you.  How you drive it, how frequently oil gets changed, etc.  If you take care of it, it will last.  <P>If I am wrong on this, please let me know!! <P>-Kemp||||reg||12
Z||000003||pontiacs4me||07-07-2001||06:02 PM||||I bought my fiero in febuary.  By march it was drivable, with a 130,000 mile 2.8 liter from a 1985 se.  The car itself is almost at 180,000.  I drove the car for about 6 weeks, then left for the power tour, drove from wichita to LA in 4 days, then straight through on the way back.  3,000 miles in 1 week, through the desert, never overheated, BTW I have a 4 banger radiator with the 2.8, with the green and white wire grounded on the fan switch.  I drive the crap out of my fiero, I deliver pizzas part time for papa johns.  Right now it is 105 degrees here, and no problems, I get about 19-20 mpg in the city, and 25-28 on the highway.  I love this car!||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||07-07-2001||06:21 PM||||You might want to ask how the miles were put on. The old story of grandma only putting 5000 miles on her 45 year old car is great, but it's usually at low speeds and the engine rotated 1.25XE499 times. If the engine had an hour meter, it would be pegged.<P>43k/14 years is about 3k/year. The seals would still have aged. Nothing new there. <P>All and all, the rule is "buy good looking first and good running second" since you will likely never paint the car or re-upholster the interior but an engine rebuild is always a flip of the wallet away.<P>Reliable? Cars break. All cars. From my experience a 43k mile Fiero is about as reliable as any other 43k mileage car. <P>Don't buy it cause you think it will go up in value. It won't. Drive it and enjoy it.  Take care of it and you'll never regret the $5500.<P>The water pump seal could go anytime with that age. The timing chains are good for 200k miles. Just listen for the sound of rocks in the engine when it's cold. <P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 07-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Old Lar||07-07-2001||11:06 PM||||My 87 just turned 87000, driving back from Illinois.  Since I bought it new, I've taken real good care of it. (Frequent oil changes)  3 batteries, 3 oil sending units,  third set of tires, Borla exhaust, O2 sensor, EGR valve and selinoid, new distributor, replaced clutch at 75000 miles and driver's seatcover back replaced. The AC was an expensive repair. Normal maintenance items wires, plugs, radiator flushes. Some painting from stone chips and paint fading.   It gets 30+ mpg at 70 mph and can still chirp the tires in second.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ElricSI||07-07-2001||11:25 PM||||It was doing pretty well until I went throught extended periods of not driving it much.  Fieros like to be driven.  Leaving it sitting on the street all winter was not good.  I don't know if it's ever gonna be mostly relaible again.  Tons of repairs in the last couple years, and more needed.  So of course I'm gonna garage it for a year.<P>------------------<BR>Proud Owner of 85 GT||||reg||9
Z||000007||MooCow||07-07-2001||11:27 PM||||My old 86 Se had 135,000 on it when I sold it an ran awsome.  No leaks at all.  It even still had the stock clutch in it.  Man I miss that car.  Talked to the owner the other day and it is still running.  The clutch finally gave out but it still runs strong and has 150,000 on it and still counting.  So it does depend on how the car was kept.  This 86 was drivin by a lady all its life.<BR> Man I miss that car<BR>                                  Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_7.jpg"> <BR>87 GT, 3.4 engine with a 4:10 tranny, and Viper Yellow!||||reg||10
Z||000008||The Fieromaster||07-07-2001||11:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GTMike:<BR><B>are fiers reliable?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well i dont have a Fiers but I do have a Fiero (had to screw with you on that spellin' error  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">!<P>Well my Fiero is reliable but I dont think it can handle my feet (heavy right one and fast moving left one!!! Hehe!<P>Da Da Da another tranny bites the dust!!!!<P>------------------<BR>      "Raced That Wrecked Better"<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.askfelons.com/fieromaster/fieromastergif1.gif">     <BR>~In Memory Of Jesse Cesek~ 1980-2000<BR>AIM: Fieromaster<BR>1985 GT ~Purple, Black and Yellow Interior(Fastback Converted)<BR>1997 Mitsu Eclipse GSX (AWD, Turbo) DOA on 12/10/2000||||reg||1
Z||000009||Wolfhound||07-08-2001||06:32 AM||||I have an 86SE 4 speed with 205,000 miles. It runs stronger than any car I have. Oil pressure is 40 psi at idle and runs 60 psi on the road. It came with a collection of receipts showing 3000 mile oil change intervals. Gas mileage is 31 highway, 21 in town. Used zero oil on it,s first change.<BR> So ,I guess the answer to your question is it depends on how the engine is treated.<BR>Coy||||reg||1
Z||000010||terryk||07-08-2001||07:30 PM||||Yep, my white 86SE 4 spd is at 194k miles and still running strong. I've got this 3.2L 9.5:1 engine sitting here waiting for the stock engine to go.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Master_Sushi||07-08-2001||07:51 PM||||I was happy with my Fieros reliability up until last weekend. I was speeding up the QEW headed to Toronto doing about 90mph. Then I hit some stop and go traffic. I noticed the temp gauge climbing up slowly, but I thought it was just because I was stuck in traffic. Then all my gauges and electrical accessories went out (no radio ,ac ,horn <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">). But the engine was still running, but now puttering really bad and coolant was spewing everywhere. So I pulled over to the shoulder and got it towed to a friends house. I’m thinking it was an electrical problem, so I’m now replacing battery, alternator, coolant and I gave it a tune up. I’m hoping this is all it needs, I am thinking about dumping my duke for a Big Red V-6 Fiero soon though  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">. This “tech-4” is not making me happy.<p>[This message has been edited by Master_Sushi (edited 07-08-2001).]||||reg||8
Z||000012||GarethWright||07-09-2001||03:01 AM||||My 87 GT has 159k on it according to the odometer, it doesnt leak anything, runs Great, but as with any car this age, things do wear out.  Normally one at a time, unless you boast that nothing is wrong with your car within ear-shot of it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000013||rogergarrison||07-09-2001||07:11 AM||||Mine now has 86K.I live in Ohio. I wouldnt even think twice about getting in it, filling the tank and leaving for california. ||||reg||1
Z||000014||AusFiero||07-09-2001||11:15 AM||||162,000 KMS here. Only things that have needed replacing are general service parts. Lets off a little blue puff of smoke on startup to let me know the valve stem seals are getting old. But has only used 1 litre of oil in 2 and a half years. Compression is consistant across the cylinders and I drive it hard so I think they last pretty well.<P>Have had a lot of other cars with less mileage die.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero5.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>Need a Subwoofer Console or Speaker Door Pods?||||reg||1
Z||000015||Ken Wittlief||07-09-2001||04:21 PM||||Hey am I the only one putting money into my Fiero? I got mine about two months ago, '85SE V6/auto with 108k on it. so far I have identified (and fixed some of):<P> leaking oil pan - PITA to replace - is patched for now<P> front caliper was dragging - rear caliper would not actuate the parking brake<P> heater core leaks<P> A/C is not working - could just be coolant leak<P> egr tube was cracked - I brazed mine - new cost $50 or more<P>  gas tank filler vent tube leaked gas on a fillup - major PITA to replace - mine is patched.<P>  the radio winks out sometimes and there is no bass and one speaker goes out, and the LED display 'wacks out'<P> the paint does not hold up well to being out in the sun all the time<P> headliners turn into dust<P>  driver's seat tends to tear<P> engine mounts tend to go soft and need replacement<P>  the can for the cruise control rusted out and had to be replaced<P> if you dont use your hand brake every day the cables will rust up and have to be replaced, and the hand brake is pretty much worthless anyway, but you gotta have it working to pass inspection.<P> the exhaust manifolds both have broken studs and both leak.<P>  from what I gather on this forum these things are all typical on a fiero - you should expect the same problems.<P>  And I would have to say most of these things are NOT typical on other cars. Ive never had any of these problems on my dodge van, any of the VWs Ive owned, or on any of my Saturns.<P>  So I would conclude the Fiero does have 'design weaknesses' that make it high maintainance - but its also a fun car to drive, and it is unique - so for me at least the pros outweight the reliability problems.<P>  but I should add that my Fiero is our third car, and I only use it for short trips around town. If its offline for a few days its not a big deal for me. If you are gonna own one, expect that you will have to work on it often.<P>  I have seen frequent posts on here for failed: fuel pumps (PITA), water pumps, clutch master/slave cylinders, suspension bushings, vacuum leaks, engines running rich<P>  and esp blown head gaskets!<P> I love my '85SE and wouldnt want to part with it, but if I were giving personal advice to a friend, I would say dont by a Fiero unless you are ready and able to work on it yourself. If you consider it a hobbie then you will love it. If you are looking for a sports car you can put gas and oil into and drive 100k carefree miles, you need to look at something else.<BR> <BR> and to show that I am not the only one with this opinion, look in an older Edmunds Used car guide that has the Fiero listed. They recommend you NOT buy one.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Alex4mula||07-09-2001||05:01 PM||||After 13 years with my Formula I learned three key areas to always keep in good shape to avoid being turned down in the street:<BR>1. Keep distributor in top shape. Heat & moisture causes havoc on pick-up coil and module dielectric. I disasemble it every 10K miles, replace pick-up coil, replace dielectric grease & clean everything. If posible I replace rotor & cap.<BR>2. Hear you water pump. Usualy it will whine before stop working. I have had 3 in my car so far.<BR>3.Keep engine clean & check/inspect cables/connections very often. That way you may prevent failures.<BR>The other tow items I have are hard to predict. One is the fuel pump and the other the radiator fan. On the fuel pump alway buy A/C Delco. Replacement brand only last about 20K miles (learned the hard way).<BR>The rest is common sense..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000017||Fierowrecker||07-09-2001||11:49 PM||||Hey Mike!<BR>I bought my first Fiero in 87, it is an 84 2M4 with over 250,000 miles of Michigan summers and winters...<BR>I retired it 2 years ago for parts, as the floor rusted out...<BR>Pontiac put in a replacement engine due to the recall at 76,000 miles, and I put in the second due to a piston that lost it's skirt...<BR>BTW, the EGR and Thermac systems were disabled on both engines...<BR>I now have 4 Fieros, 2 running and 2 for parts...<BR>Break downs have been few and minor, except for the piston loosing it's skirt!<BR>When that happened, the piston went sideways and cracked the block!!!<BR>Lots of steam from the exhaust!!!<BR>I drive 50 miles to and from work, and a lot of play time adds up too, so I need a reliable car...<BR>My Fieros fill the bill, now my wife drives the second road car to and from work...<BR>I would recomend you do your own maintenance, if you want reliable transportation... <BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000018||RoadRocket||07-10-2001||01:02 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>Hey am I the only one putting money into my Fiero? I got mine about two months ago, '85SE V6/auto with 108k on it. so far I have identified (and fixed some of):<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Nope. You're not the only one. Mine has become a bit expensive of late. <B>But</B> it has been a pretty dependable car up until now. It's still dependable, just needs a little more TLC than it used to.<P>It's an 88 Coupe with 124k miles on it, that sat for the past four years. <BR><P>------------------<BR>Ed Dana<BR>88 Coupe.||||reg||1
Z||000019||lowCG||07-10-2001||02:48 AM||||My '87 S/E has been great for reliability;bought it with 121K on the clock,had cracked leaky exhaust manifolds,stuck EGR,yet ran great,drove it across the country with only an oil change.<BR>Swapped in a 3.4,4 cam V6,and drove it back again just days after the swap.<BR>I would recommend getting a car that's closest to the way you want it cosmetically;a neglected car will have it's knobs and latches kicking around on the floor,yet still run alright,and you don't seem to find them the other way all that often.||||reg||1
